WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:06.480
Okay, so now let's check the easiest syntax ever.

00:06.480 --> 00:09.280
So for the temporary table the syntax is going to look like this.

00:09.640 --> 00:12.240
You're going to have like a query select from where.

00:12.240 --> 00:17.880
And as we learned in the CTAs, if you go and say into then the table name, it's going to go and create

00:17.880 --> 00:19.120
a physical new table.

00:19.120 --> 00:24.360
But now if you want it as a temporary table, what you're going to do, you're going to just put hash

00:24.360 --> 00:29.680
before the name of the table, then SQL can understand okay, now we are talking about temporary table

00:29.680 --> 00:33.000
and the database is going to store it in that temporary storage.

00:33.000 --> 00:34.320
So it is very simple.

00:34.520 --> 00:36.800
This is the syntax of that temporary tables.

00:36.800 --> 00:39.880
So so far we have learned that we have a database called sales DB.

00:39.880 --> 00:46.000
And inside it we can find the tables that we have created, the customers employees orders and so on.

00:46.080 --> 00:48.520
Those are our tables and they are always there.

00:48.560 --> 00:53.400
Like if you go and close everything and then start it or in the next day, you're going to find always

00:53.400 --> 00:57.720
those tables with the same data so they can exist as long as you are not dropping them.

00:58.400 --> 01:01.680
Now the question is, where do we find that temporary tables?

01:01.680 --> 01:07.680
Well, as we learned, if you go over here at the system databases you will find multiple databases

01:07.730 --> 01:11.450
from the SQL server, and normally only the database administrator.

01:11.610 --> 01:15.650
Has an access to this and one of those databases called Tempdb.

01:15.690 --> 01:17.050
Temporary database.

01:17.050 --> 01:18.210
So let's go inside it.

01:18.250 --> 01:19.850
Now we can find multiple objects.

01:19.850 --> 01:23.050
And one of them we can find here the temporary tables.

01:23.090 --> 01:26.770
And now of course we don't have anything inside it because we didn't create anything.

01:26.770 --> 01:28.290
So let's go and create one.

01:28.290 --> 01:31.770
We have already an open session, an active session with the SQL server.

01:31.810 --> 01:36.370
As you can see here, we are connected to the database and we can start creating temporary tables.

01:36.370 --> 01:37.610
So now what is the plan.

01:37.930 --> 01:43.490
I would like now to do few modifications on the table orders, but I will not do it directly at the

01:43.490 --> 01:44.450
table orders.

01:44.450 --> 01:49.650
I would like to take a copy from the sales db and create from it a temporary table.

01:49.650 --> 01:51.170
So let's go and do that.

01:51.210 --> 01:51.770
What do we need?

01:51.810 --> 01:53.450
First we need a query.

01:53.450 --> 01:59.610
So I would like to select everything all the columns all the rows from the table orders.

01:59.610 --> 02:02.330
So from sales orders.

02:02.610 --> 02:04.570
So this is my query now.

02:04.570 --> 02:05.810
So far nothing is created.

02:05.810 --> 02:07.610
We have only select statements.

02:07.610 --> 02:12.730
But now in order to create a temporary table what we're going to do we're going to put a statement between

02:12.730 --> 02:14.210
the select and from.

02:14.210 --> 02:20.630
So exactly before the from go over here and say into then in order to make sure it is a temporary table,

02:20.630 --> 02:22.710
we use hash and then the table name.

02:22.710 --> 02:24.790
So we're going to call it orders.

02:24.790 --> 02:25.510
So that's it.

02:25.550 --> 02:26.630
We have our query.

02:26.630 --> 02:28.710
And in between we have the into.

02:28.750 --> 02:32.390
And make sure you are using hash in order to be a temporary table.

02:32.390 --> 02:34.070
So let's go and execute it.

02:34.070 --> 02:37.350
And now we can see that ten rows are affected and we don't have any error.

02:37.390 --> 02:41.950
And now of course we cannot see it yet because we have to go and refresh the object explorer.

02:41.950 --> 02:43.190
So let's go and do that.

02:43.590 --> 02:45.310
And now let's expand it.

02:45.310 --> 02:47.630
And now we can see our temporary tables.

02:47.630 --> 02:49.830
As you can see it is at the schema dbo.

02:49.870 --> 02:51.710
Because we haven't defined any schema.

02:51.710 --> 02:54.190
And this is the default one from the database.

02:54.190 --> 02:54.870
So nice.

02:54.870 --> 02:56.030
Now we have the table.

02:56.070 --> 02:57.750
And let's go and check a few stuff.

02:57.750 --> 02:59.790
So let's go and select the table itself.

02:59.790 --> 03:05.430
So select star from and make sure to say hash orders.

03:05.470 --> 03:06.710
Let's go and select it.

03:06.750 --> 03:11.510
And now we are getting the data from the temporary table and not from the original table.

03:11.510 --> 03:14.190
The orders in the database sales DB.

03:14.190 --> 03:17.390
So all those informations comes from the temporary table.

03:17.430 --> 03:22.200
Now of course you can do whatever you want to this temporary table because it's not that important and

03:22.200 --> 03:23.840
it's anyway going to get deleted.

03:23.840 --> 03:29.920
So let's say that I would like to delete all the orders where the order status equal to delivered.

03:29.920 --> 03:31.080
So let's go and do that.

03:31.080 --> 03:36.200
So what we're going to do delete from our hash orders.

03:36.200 --> 03:38.560
So make sure you are selecting the temporary table.

03:38.600 --> 03:46.040
And then where are we going to say the order status equal to what I say.

03:46.080 --> 03:46.640
Delivered.

03:46.680 --> 03:46.920
Yeah.

03:46.960 --> 03:47.600
Delivered.

03:47.920 --> 03:49.800
So delivered like this.

03:49.800 --> 03:51.680
Let's go and execute it okay.

03:51.720 --> 03:54.240
With that it says five rows are affected.

03:54.560 --> 03:56.280
Let's go and select it again.

03:56.280 --> 04:03.120
So select from orders and let's check that.

04:03.120 --> 04:04.960
So as you can see now we don't have all orders.

04:04.960 --> 04:08.000
We have only the orders where the status equal to shipped.

04:08.000 --> 04:10.480
So all delivered orders are removed.

04:10.480 --> 04:12.440
And now we can do whatever we want to this copy.

04:12.440 --> 04:13.320
We can analyze it.

04:13.360 --> 04:14.520
We can modify it.

04:14.560 --> 04:19.440
We can go and insert a new data so we can do whatever manipulation we want on this copy.

04:19.480 --> 04:21.960
And now if you say, you know what, I like these results.

04:21.960 --> 04:26.600
And I would like to have it not only during the session, maybe I'm going to need it for tomorrow or

04:26.600 --> 04:27.320
something.

04:27.320 --> 04:29.730
So now what we're going to do, to do, we're going to do the exact opposite.

04:29.770 --> 04:35.730
We're going to now store the result of that temporary table back to our database, so that we don't

04:35.730 --> 04:37.450
lose this intermediate result.

04:37.490 --> 04:44.050
So in order to do that we're going to say into and then make sure to specify the sales dots because

04:44.050 --> 04:46.010
we want to select the correct schema.

04:46.010 --> 04:48.370
And then let's say it is orders.

04:48.490 --> 04:50.930
And I'm going to call it test like this.

04:50.930 --> 04:52.890
So let's go and execute it.

04:52.890 --> 04:55.090
So it says five rows are affected.

04:55.330 --> 04:58.290
Now we have to see those informations in the sales DB.

04:58.410 --> 05:00.050
We still don't have this table over here.

05:00.050 --> 05:03.250
So right click on the DB and then refresh it.

05:03.250 --> 05:05.050
So let's go again to the tables.

05:05.090 --> 05:08.250
And now you can see we have our new table orders test.

05:08.250 --> 05:09.890
So it is amazing right.

05:09.890 --> 05:16.130
What we have done is we have took a copy from the original table orders to a temporary space.

05:16.130 --> 05:21.010
We have done some modifications and play with the data, and we have done some analysis.

05:21.130 --> 05:28.330
And then the end result of our temporary table, we have loaded back to another new table called orders

05:28.330 --> 05:31.490
test in order maybe in the next day to keep working on it.

05:31.490 --> 05:35.790
So it is a really nice way to do changes in place where you say you know what?

05:35.830 --> 05:36.790
It is temporary.

05:36.790 --> 05:38.910
And whatever mistakes you make, it's okay.

05:38.950 --> 05:40.350
It is like playground.

05:40.350 --> 05:45.190
So now we still have an active session with the database, and our temporary table is going to be always

05:45.190 --> 05:45.550
here.

05:45.590 --> 05:48.470
Now let's see what can happen if we end our session.

05:48.470 --> 05:51.870
So in order to do that let's go and just close everything.

05:51.870 --> 05:56.350
So I will just close and we'll not store anything.

05:56.750 --> 05:59.070
So with that we have now ended the session.

05:59.070 --> 06:02.750
Let's go and start it again and see whether we still have the temporary table.

06:02.750 --> 06:06.150
So we have now again to connect to the SQL server.

06:06.150 --> 06:08.070
And now we have another session.

06:08.070 --> 06:10.790
So that means the old session is already lost.

06:10.790 --> 06:15.350
Let's go to the databases to the system databases to the temp DB.

06:15.510 --> 06:17.390
And let's go to the temporary tables.

06:17.430 --> 06:23.630
As you can see, the database already cleaned up everything and this space is again empty for any new

06:23.670 --> 06:25.310
temporary table that I'm going to create.

06:25.310 --> 06:28.950
So as you can see, once you close the session everything going to get lost.

06:28.990 --> 06:30.750
Now let's go back to our sales DB.

06:30.790 --> 06:32.390
Over here to the tables.

06:32.390 --> 06:35.790
We can see the table that we have created orders, tests.

06:35.790 --> 06:39.590
It is still living here and still has the data that we have created.

06:39.590 --> 06:42.750
So this is how things works with the temporary tables in SQL.
